            "A wound is a break in the continuity of the skin or a deeper tissue, and the nurse describes it by its cause — a surgical <strong>incision</strong>, a <strong>laceration</strong> with torn edges, an <strong>abrasion</strong> of the surface, a <strong>puncture</strong> that is deeper than it is wide and traps bacteria, a <strong>contusion</strong> without a break, an <strong>avulsion</strong> where tissue is torn away, a <strong>crush</strong>, a <strong>bite</strong> (always contaminated), a <strong>gunshot</strong> wound with its hidden track — and by its cleanliness, because the classification predicts infection: a <strong>clean</strong> wound (an elective hernia repair; about 2 % infect), <strong>clean-contaminated</strong> (a controlled opening of the gut, airway or urinary tract; about 10 %), <strong>contaminated</strong> (fresh trauma, gross spillage, a major break in technique; about 20 %) and <strong>dirty</strong> (old trauma, pus, a perforated viscus; about 40 %). Healing runs through <strong>four overlapping phases</strong>: <strong>haemostasis</strong> within minutes, as vessels constrict and a platelet plug and clot seal the gap; <strong>inflammation</strong> from day 0 to about day 4, when the vessels dilate and leak, neutrophils and macrophages clear debris and bacteria, and the wound is red, warm, swollen and sore — the five cardinal signs are the repair arriving, not a disaster, and the nurse expects them for the first two or three days; <strong>proliferation</strong> from about day 3 to day 21, when fibroblasts lay down collagen, new capillaries sprout into the beefy red <strong>granulation tissue</strong>, the wound <strong>contracts</strong> and the epithelium creeps across from the edges; and <strong>maturation</strong>, from three weeks to two years, as collagen is remodelled, the scar pales and flattens and the tissue regains at most about <strong>80 % of its original strength</strong> — which is why the scar is the place a hernia appears and why the patient lifts nothing heavy for weeks.",
