            "Every wound assessment answers the same questions and records the answers so that the next nurse can see whether it is better or worse: the <strong>site</strong> and cause; the <strong>size</strong> — length, width and depth in centimetres, with any <strong>undermining</strong> or sinus probed gently and recorded by the clock face; the <strong>tissue</strong> on the bed by colour — <strong>black</strong> necrotic eschar, <strong>yellow</strong> slough, <strong>red</strong> granulation, <strong>pink</strong> new epithelium, and a green discharge that suggests <em>Pseudomonas</em> — with the proportion of each estimated, because black to yellow to red to pink is the direction of healing and a wound going the other way is deteriorating; the <strong>exudate</strong> — none, low, moderate or heavy, and serous (clear straw), serosanguinous (pink), sanguinous (bloody) or purulent (thick, coloured, offensive); the <strong>odour</strong>; the <strong>edges</strong> — advancing, rolled, undermined, macerated; the <strong>surrounding skin</strong> — maceration from moisture, cellulitis, eczema, callus; the <strong>pain</strong>; and the <strong>signs of infection</strong>. All chronic wounds are <strong>colonised</strong> and a swab of an open wound will always grow something, so a swab is taken only when there are clinical signs of <strong>infection</strong> — pain that increases rather than settles, spreading redness, heat and swelling, pus, a new smell, friable bleeding granulation, pocketing or breakdown, delayed healing, fever and a rising pulse — and it is taken from cleaned viable tissue, not from pus or slough. A tracing, a photograph with the patient's consent, or at least a drawing and the measurements go in the record every time the dressing is changed.",
