            "Burns in Ghana come from the kitchen and the lamp: <strong>scalds</strong> from pots of soup, boiling water and hot oil pulled down by toddlers, <strong>flame burns</strong> from kerosene lamps and stoves, charcoal fires, bush burning, gas cylinder leaks and explosions and loose clothing catching fire, <strong>electrical burns</strong> from illegal connections and bare wires, <strong>chemical burns</strong> from caustic soda used in soap-making and from acid attacks, and the burns of the person with epilepsy who falls into the cooking fire — which is why every patient with epilepsy is taught to cook sitting down, away from an open flame. Most harm is done twice, once by the fire and once by what is put on the burn afterwards: <strong>palm oil, shea butter, toothpaste, raw egg, powdered herbs, engine oil, soil, ash and ice</strong> all hold the heat in, introduce infection, deepen the burn and hide its depth from the person who must assess it. The nurse <strong>asks what was applied</strong>, without scolding — she needs to know — and teaches the family what to do next time.",
            "<strong>First aid</strong> is simple and is what the examiner asks: <strong>stop the burning</strong> — stop, drop and roll, smother the flames, switch off the electricity before touching the patient, brush off a dry chemical powder before wetting it; <strong>remove</strong> clothing and jewellery that is not stuck before the swelling starts (a ring on a burned finger becomes a tourniquet within an hour); <strong>cool the burn with cool running water for twenty minutes</strong> — not ice, which causes a second cold injury, and not for longer in a child or a large burn, because the patient must be <strong>kept warm</strong> while the burn is cooled; cooling still helps up to about three hours after the injury; <strong>cover</strong> with a clean non-fluffy cloth or cling film laid on, not wrapped tightly round a limb; give analgesia; and get the patient to a facility. A <strong>chemical burn</strong> is irrigated with copious running water for at least 20–30 minutes (longer for alkali and for the eye, which is irrigated until the pH is neutral) with contaminated clothing removed and the irrigating water kept off unburned skin, and nothing is added to ‘neutralise’ it, because the reaction generates heat. An <strong>electrical burn</strong> has a small entry and exit wound and a great deal of destroyed muscle between them, so the patient needs an ECG and cardiac monitoring, a watch for <strong>myoglobin</strong> in dark urine that needs a higher urine output, and a watch for compartment syndrome.",
            "The patient burned in an <strong>enclosed space</strong>, with burns of the face, <strong>singed nasal hair or eyebrows</strong>, <strong>soot in the mouth, nose or sputum</strong>, a <strong>hoarse voice</strong>, a cough, stridor or wheeze, or a reduced level of consciousness, has an <strong>inhalation injury</strong> until proved otherwise: the airway swells over the following hours and closes, so the anaesthetist is called early, while intubation is still possible, and the patient is sat up and given humidified high-flow oxygen. <strong>Carbon monoxide</strong> from the fire binds haemoglobin and causes headache, confusion, drowsiness and collapse with a <strong>pulse oximeter reading that is falsely normal</strong>, so the oximeter is not believed in a fire victim and <strong>100 % oxygen</strong> is given by a non-rebreathing mask until the carboxyhaemoglobin is known to be low.",
